\f0\fs24 \cf0 Story 1 of Fontiveros - like I say, it's a village out of children's story book \
\
 A man came for the exhibition on Day 1 and he said he would like me to see his paintings too. \
\
On Day 2, he arrived to pick us up, despite living only a short distance away. I went with Jos\'e9, entering a house that seemed to belong in a children\'92s fable. The garden was a quiet symphony of textures\'97the silver-green needles of pine, a humming fountain, and the scent of rosemary and grapevines.\
\
Upon entering, I was stunned by his father\'92s legacy. A man who worked in the maintenance department of the Madrid Metro had spent his free time sculpting\'97including a massive, three-foot-tall ship of meticulous longing. In the hall there was one ship, then Pablo opened a room and there were about 6 of them, the last unfinished.\
He never sold a single piece, and his son maintains that tradition today. The father\'92s workshop remains a sacred site, with hammers and screwdrivers laid out as if he might return at any moment to brush the sawdust from his sleeves.\
\
The son, Pablo, is equally remarkable. He refused to retire at 60, continuing his career until he was 80. Only in these last fifteen years has he taken up sketching, birthing a new world into existence. He has held a few exhibitions but refuses to sell his work. He lives alone in that large, four-bedroom house, still driving a car and a motorbike from an archaic era.\
Time was short, but he is returning today with a folder to reveal more of his paintings.\
\
Meet Pablo, now he is 90 years old}